Give be-cpp's runtime std/net, through a pluggable client (temper::core::net::set_client) whose default is libcurl's multi interface on a temper-io thread, where the build defines TEMPER_NET_CURL; and let other threads post work back to the async queue, which waits while any hold is out.
Upstream: not yet; to offer Kotlin Temper (Kotlin's C++ has no std/net).

net.hpp is new, and only std/net's generated net.cpp (and host code that
plugs in a client) includes it. A client responds from any thread; the
response is posted (async_post) to the thread that runs Temper code, where
it settles the promise, which queues the waiters. Requests and responses
hold std::strings, for host code and other threads; sendRequest and the
NetResponse take and give Temper's Str. Without a client, a
request gives a broken promise, as std says a backend that can't send
gives, and says how to plug one in.

